---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const stages = [
  { id: 'A', name: 'Word in context', done: false,
    what: 'Take a sentence, split it into words, and choose the right analysis for each one in context rather than returning every possibility.',
    why: 'Tamil surface forms are massively ambiguous in isolation and context resolves most of it. Nothing further is possible without this.' },
  { id: 'B', name: 'Structure', done: false,
    what: 'Parse the sentence: which word governs which.',
    why: 'It lets us ask what grammatical slot comes next, rather than what token comes next.' },
  { id: 'C', name: 'The realizer', done: false,
    what: 'Give it a lemma and a bundle of grammatical features, get back the correct surface form. Join words with the right புணர்ச்சி.',
    why: 'This needs no training at all. The transducer already runs in this direction and we simply do not use it. Highest value for the least work in this whole list.' },
  { id: 'D', name: 'The validator', done: false,
    what: 'Check Tamil that something else produced: is every form parseable, does agreement hold, is case government right? Return corrections with the rule that was broken.',
    why: 'This is a Tamil grammar checker, and it is plausibly wanted by more people than the analyser is.' },
  { id: 'E', name: 'The first predictor', done: false,
    what: 'De-agglutinate a corpus into sequences of meaningful units, then train a modest model over those units to predict the next one.',
    why: 'Laptop scale, no specialised hardware, weeks rather than months. It is a Tamil next-word predictor that reasons over morphemes rather than fragments.' },
  { id: 'F', name: 'Tokenizer, then a model', done: false,
    what: 'A tokenizer whose units are morphemes, and then a small Tamil model trained with it.',
    why: 'The segmentation data that trains the tokenizer is what the server has been accumulating since the beginning.' },
];
---
<Base
  title="The horizon"
  description="From a word analyser to a Tamil-native model: the stages, the flywheel that feeds them, and the two questions we have to settle first."
  derivesFrom={[
    { label: 'DESIGN.md §8', href: 'https://github.com/ief-global/thamizh-mcp-design/blob/main/DESIGN.md' },
    { label: 'DECISIONS.md D-006', href: 'https://github.com/ief-global/thamizh-mcp-design/blob/main/DECISIONS.md' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="pagehead">
      <p class="kicker">Horizon</p>
      <h1 class="ta">தமிழ் மொழி மாதிரி <span class="en">a Tamil-native model</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Written down so that today's choices do not accidentally rule it out. <b>None of this is
        scheduled</b>, and this page would be dishonest if it read like a plan.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Why data, and not a bigger model</h2>
      <p>
        Tamil is short of machine-readable linguistic data rather than of literature. So the useful
        move is to make the missing data, and the way to make it without funding a labelling project
        is to produce it as a by-product of something people already want.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every analysis the server resolves is stored with its sources and the date. Verified,
        provenance-tagged, and growing whenever anyone uses the tool.
      </p>

      <ol class="flywheel">
        <li><b>Somebody asks</b> about a Tamil word.</li>
        <li><b>The engine answers</b> from cited sources, or returns an honest gap.</li>
        <li><b>The answer is stored</b> with every claim's provenance attached.</li>
        <li><b>Curation</b> separates verified from generated from disputed, and drops anything a licence will not let us publish.</li>
        <li><b>Published datasets</b> of exactly the kinds that do not currently exist for Tamil.</li>
        <li><b>Better Tamil tooling</b>, ours and other people's, which brings more use, which returns to step one.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="src">
        The eval fixtures are flagged in the store so they can never leak into a published dataset. A
        benchmark is worth nothing once the thing being measured has read it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Predicting the next Tamil word is three problems</h2>
      <p>Only one of them actually needs a trained model, which is the observation this whole section rests on:</p>
      <div class="three">
        <div class="p3"><p class="pq">Which idea comes next?</p><p class="pa">Meaning and discourse. This genuinely needs a trained model.</p></div>
        <div class="p3"><p class="pq">What grammatical shape must it take?</p><p class="pa">Agreement, case government, tense chaining. Largely rule-governed, and classical grammar plus a treebank cover most of it.</p></div>
        <div class="p3"><p class="pq">What string is that, exactly?</p><p class="pa">Inflection and <span class="ta">புணர்ச்சி</span>. Fully deterministic, and we already own the machinery.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p>
        So the target is not a Tamil model that does everything. It is a pairing: a large model
        proposes a word and its grammatical features, and this engine turns that into correct Tamil.
        The hard, error-prone morphology gets handled by rules that cannot hallucinate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The stages</h2>
      <p>
        Each one is independently useful, and each is a prerequisite for the next. If the last stage
        never happens, everything before it still stands on its own.
      </p>
      <ol class="stages">
        {stages.map((s) => (
          <li class="stage">
            <p class="sid">{s.id}</p>
            <div>
              <p class="sn">{s.name}</p>
              <p class="sw ta">{s.what}</p>
              <p class="sy">{s.why}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="note">
        <b>Stage C before stage E, always.</b> A predictor without a realizer just produces more
        fragmented output, which is the problem we started with.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The cheap experiment that would settle it</h2>
      <p>
        Before committing to any of the expensive stages, there is a few days of work that would tell
        us whether the whole thesis holds: take Tamil Wikipedia, split it into morphemes with the
        analyser we already have, then train two small models, one over ordinary tokens and one over
        morphemes, and compare how well each predicts the next unit.
      </p>
      <p>
        If morphemes win clearly, the long-term argument is proved and it is a publishable result
        nobody has produced. If they do not, we have saved a year. It is the highest information per
        hour available to this project, and it needs no funding.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Two questions we have to answer before any of this</h2>
      <dl class="landmines">
        <dt>Honest gaps do not survive generation</dt>
        <dd class="ta">
          "No attested analysis" is a fine answer for an analyser. In the middle of a sentence it is
          not, because something has to be produced. Refuse the whole sentence? Emit with a
          confidence flag? Fall back and mark it ungrounded? Whichever we choose, choosing it late
          means the honesty rule erodes quietly rather than being changed on purpose.
        </dd>
        <dt>Rule-attested is not source-attested</dt>
        <dd class="ta">
          A realizer necessarily produces correct Tamil forms that no dictionary has ever recorded.
          That is fine, and it is a different kind of evidence from a word somebody attested. The
          rule against inventing words has to be scoped narrowly to <b>coining a new word</b>, or it
          will accidentally forbid inflecting an existing one.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When this becomes real</h2>
      <p>
        There is a written trigger, so the decision is not made on enthusiasm: roughly a hundred
        thousand verified records in the corpus, <b>and</b> evaluation that runs as routine rather
        than as an event. Until both hold, this stays where it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything on <a href="/status">the current status page</a> is what produces those two
        conditions. That is the actual relationship between the near work and the far work, and it is
        why the far work is not being solutioned now.
      </p>
      <p class="onward"><a href="/sources">What we still need to get there &rarr;</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .pagehead { margin: clamp(40px, 7vh, 72px) 0 0; }
  .pagehead h1 { font-size: clamp(26px, 4.2vw, 42px); line-height: 1.15; margin: 12px 0 0; }
  .pagehead h1 .en { font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 17px; color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 400; margin-left: 12px; }
  .lede { font-size: 19px; color: var(--muted); max-width: 60ch; margin: 20px 0 0; }
  .src { font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 14px; }

  .flywheel { margin: 22px 0 0; padding-left: 22px; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .flywheel li { margin: 9px 0; }

  .three { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr));
    gap: 1px; background: var(--line); border: 1px solid var(--line); margin: 22px 0 0; }
  .p3 { background: var(--card); padding: 15px 17px; }
  .pq { margin: 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent2); }
  .pa { margin: 9px 0 0; font-size: 14.5px; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5; }

  .stages { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 22px 0 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }
  .stage { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 46px 1fr; gap: 16px; border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 5px solid var(--accent2); background: var(--card); padding: 15px 18px; }
  .sid { font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 26px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--line); margin: 0; line-height: 1; }
  .sn { font-size: 18px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; }
  .sw { margin: 8px 0 0; font-size: 15px; }
  .sy { margin: 8px 0 0; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted); font-family: var(--ui); line-height: 1.5; }

  .landmines dt { font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--warn); font-weight: 600; margin-top: 24px; }
  .landmines dd { margin: 10px 0 0; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .onward { font-family: var(--ui); margin-top: 30px; }
</style>
